            <div xml:id="d670">

                <pb facs="0896" n="794" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg794"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/1556: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United
                        Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-10-04T18:00:00-05:00">October 4, 1935—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>285. Your 493, October 4, 4 p.m. Please ascertain immediately if possible the
                    basis for the British opinion that a state of war does not exist. In particular,
                    is this attitude based upon legal considerations that relate to belligerent
                    rights, or is it only the result of practical considerations pending the Council
                    meeting. Any additional information along these lines will be much
                    appreciated.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
